          "source_quote": "οὐκ αὐτὸς αἴτιός ἐστιν ὢν αὕτη τίκτει κακῶν;",
          "english_quote": "is he not himself the cause, being—this matter gives birth—of evils?",
          "issue": "The surviving words are represented mechanically, but the English does not convey an intelligible construction. Reading ὢν as the printed participle does not establish how αὕτη τίκτει connects with αἴτιός and κακῶν. The draft correctly acknowledges this unresolved problem, but disclosure cannot make the dash-based wording a defensible translation. The block concerns unsupported syntactic meaning, not unverified print or the separately preserved five- and six-dot lacunae.",
          "suggested_revision": "Establish a defensible construction for the supplied reading, or submit a separately justified editorial correction for fresh review. Changing the printed ὢν to the relative ὧν would permit the candidate 'is he not himself the cause of the evils that this matter gives birth to?' That is a proposed correction, not the supplied reading or an accepted replacement; the adjacent context identifies matter but does not establish this correction.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὗ εἵνεκεν θάνατος παρεισῆλθε",
          "english_quote": "on whose account death entered",
          "issue": "The personal antecedent is defensible, but the note describes οὗ as masculine without preserving its viable neuter interpretation. It can refer to the preceding fact that the human being sinned, yielding 'because of which death entered.' This alternative materially affects the expressed causal connection and is supported by the subsequent explanation of susceptibility to suffering through sin.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the main rendering if desired, but revise the note to disclose both readings: masculine οὗ referring to the immediately preceding human being, and neuter οὗ referring to the preceding event of sinning. Explain that proximity to ὁ ἄνθρωπος motivates the main choice, while the causal argument supports the alternative 'because of which death entered.'",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
